// URLs, in the two grammars the web has:
//   RFC 3986, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986
//   WHATWG URL Standard, https://url.spec.whatwg.org/
//
// Taking one apart, putting it back, resolving a relative one against a base,
// and the percent coding that lets any octet travel inside one. A form body, a
// request target, a `Location` and a data URL all obey this grammar.
//
// Percent coding takes the component as a parameter. Which octets may stand for
// themselves is RFC 3986's answer; three callers writing that list by hand is
// three chances to get it wrong.
//
// The two grammars disagree about what a string means — a special scheme's
// default port, a backslash, a host written as a number — so the reader takes
// the one to use. They agree about the shape of the answer: one `Url`, holding
// the scheme, the credentials, the host, the port, the path, the query and the
// fragment. Host names are internationalised through `moonurl/idna`, so a name
// is a word in any script rather than a run of letters.
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/encoding/utf8",
  "moonbitstack/moonurl/idna",
}
